  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nAlright, move on, nothing to see here... besides being a great\nmovie quote also sums up our weather over the next 7+ days. If\nyou've been outside today, you've likely noticed a decidedly \ndrier airmass is in place and that is thanks to the cold front \nthat cleared the MPX area to the southeast this morning. We're \nseeing high pressure move in from Montana, with dewpoints in the\nDakotas into northern MN down in the 30s. As this high moves \nin, we'll see skies gradually clear out, which will set up a \ncool start to your Friday, with lows in the upper 30s to mid \n40s.\n\nSoutherly winds develop late in the day on Thursday as the high \nmoves into WI/IL. These southerly winds will remain in place \nthrough the day on Saturday before our next cold front moves \nthrough Saturday night. Eventually, these southerly winds will \nbring a chance at seeing some highs in the 70s on Saturday in \nwestern MN, but the big thing with this front is it represents \nour only, and rather meager, chance at rain over the next 7 \ndays. The upper wave driving this front will be going across \nCanada, with an extension down into MN. So the forcing is better\nto the north. Moisture is lack luster as we never really \nrecover our moisture feed from today's frontal passage. Forecast\nsoundings show very dry air in the lowest 8k feet or so of the \natmosphere. So you add in weak forcing, weak moisture, no \nconvective instability, and the inverted-v soundings and it all \nadds up to a pretty minor rain event. We expect scattered \nshowers to move through with the front Saturday night and did \nsee the NBM bump up PoPs into the 30s, but amounts will be under\na tenth of an inch. The good news is these rain chances will \ncome long after any Little Brown Jugs are earned.\n\nBehind this front, we get high pressure and pleasant weather for \nSunday, with excellent weather conditions for things like any \nruns or tailgating adventures you may have planned for what will\nbe a busy weekend of events in the Twin Cities.\n\nNext week, we'll see above normal heights spread across the\nCONUS, with the upper jet retreating into Canada. This is a \nclassic setup for seeing mild and dry Pacific airmasses \noverspreading the CONUS and that's exactly what we'll see. The \nhigh pressure from Sunday will be in the Great Lakes by the \nstart of next week, with warm southerly winds overspreading the \nregion. Add in a shortwave moving across Canada mid-week helping\nto draw warm air north and we have what should be a beautiful \nstretch of weather next week, with highs in the 70s starting \nTuesday. There are no indications of any stronger fronts and \nchanges to this airmass until next weekend, though right now, \ndeterministic, machine learning, and ensemble models alike show \nthis being another dry fropa.",
